# Solderi project authoring

When creating or changing a Solderi project, walkthrough, BOM, wiring diagram, firmware, or compatibility requirement:

- Read and follow `docs/PROJECT_AUTHORING.md` before editing.
- Use the existing walkthrough, catalogue, wiring-layout, and firmware files. A growing-sketch project stays a growing sketch. A single-firmware project stays one program. Do not invent a new project shape.
- Include every physical part the build needs, with a catalogue id and a quantity, so the hardware list can be built as written.
- Check the wiring table, diagram pins, instructions, code, and compatibility rows against each other. The same part and the same connection must use the same names.
- Run `npm run test:compatibility`. `localAuthoredProjects` in `lib/authored-projects.ts` runs `reviewAuthoredProject` on every file in `constants/walkthroughs/`.
- When the project has one firmware sketch and a matching toolchain is installed, compile that sketch and record the result. If the toolchain is missing, say compilation was not run.
- Leave a new project unpublished. Publishing is a separate review.
- Say when behaviour was not run on a bench, and name any missing cover or other asset.
- When creating a project or changing its thumbnail, read and follow `docs/PROJECT_THUMBNAILS.md`. Match the existing workbench photographs. Show that project’s hardware. Register the JPEG on its own `PROJECT_IMAGES` key. A generated cover is not a bench test and is not the wiring diagram.
- A passing structural check does not prove the project works physically.
- Do not approve a component substitution. A substitution needs its own compatibility review id.

```ts
// Bad: mark a substitute as approved while writing the project
{ policy: 'direct', approvedReviewIds: [''] }

// Good: keep the requirement exact until a separate review exists
{ policy: 'exact', approvedReviewIds: [] }
```
